WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.660
A protocol parser transforms received bytes into application meaning.

2
00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:14.200
It takes raw input and decides where a message begins, which fields exist, which values are valid, and which application action may follow.

3
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:18.460
That makes the parser one of the most important trust boundaries in a C program.

4
00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:27.220
Protocol input may come from another process, device, service, tenant, network zone, or user-controlled source.

5
00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:32.200
Even when the source is expected, the bytes are still external to the receiving component.

6
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:41.820
They can be incomplete, outdated, malformed, unsupported, repeated, or valid for a different state than the one the application is currently in.

7
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:48.980
A defensive parser treats input as untrusted until structure, state, and value checks succeed.

8
00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:55.240
A field is not a trusted length simply because bytes exist where a length is expected.

9
00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:59.980
A command is not authorized simply because the command identifier is recognized.

10
00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:03.360
Each piece of meaning is earned by validation.

11
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:07.000
Parser defects can affect more than immediate decoding.

12
00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:16.220
They can influence memory safety, authorization decisions, business logic, service stability, session state, and data integrity.

13
00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.720
A parser that accepts the wrong structure may hand later code a trusted-looking object that should never have existed.

14
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:31.800
Secure parsing begins with a clear boundary between raw input, decoded fields, validated messages, and trusted application objects.

15
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:35.500
That separation keeps parser responsibilities visible.

16
00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:41.760
It also gives reviewers a practical way to ask whether each transition is justified by an explicit rule.
